<div id="article-body">

<p id="elk-e69ac6d8-b824-11f1-93b1-17a403735973">In 2024, Microsoft and <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/processors/qualcomm" data-auto-tag-linker="true" data-url="https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/processors/qualcomm" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-redirect="https://www.windowscentral.com/tag/qualcomm" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/processors/qualcomm">Qualcomm</a> kicked off a new category of Windows hardware <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.windowscentral.com/microsoft/windows/copilot-plus-pc-faq" data-url="https://www.windowscentral.com/microsoft/windows/copilot-plus-pc-faq" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.windowscentral.com/microsoft/windows/copilot-plus-pc-faq">it called "Copilot+ PCs"</a> that set the baseline for a new wave of AI-first computers, and launched with exclusive features that would not be made available on PCs that didn't meet a new set of system requirements.</p><p>The launch didn't go smoothly. Copilot+ PCs were met with immediate backlash from security researchers after <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.windowscentral.com/microsoft/windows-11/why-this-windows-recall-feature-raises-real-security-questions" data-url="https://www.windowscentral.com/microsoft/windows-11/why-this-windows-recall-feature-raises-real-security-questions" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.windowscentral.com/microsoft/windows-11/why-this-windows-recall-feature-raises-real-security-questions">discovering that Copilot+ PCs blockbuster feature, Recall, was woefully insecure.</a> This forced the company to delay Recall and ship this new category of devices without its leading <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.windowscentral.com/artificial-intelligence" data-auto-tag-linker="true" data-url="https://www.windowscentral.com/artificial-intelligence" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-redirect="https://www.windowscentral.com/tag/artificial-intelligence" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.windowscentral.com/artificial-intelligence">AI</a> experience.</p><p id="elk-e69ac6d8-b824-11f1-93b1-17a403735973-2">This pretty much tarnished the Copilot+ PC brand, and over the last two years more and more OEMs have dropped the moniker from marketing materials and product names. In fact, even Microsoft has seemingly stopped mentioning it.</p><p>I've noticed that none of the Surface PCs launched in 2026 include the Copilot+ PC moniker in their product names, unlike the Surface PCs that launched in 2025 and before. Now, you have to go digging to find any mention of Copilot+ compatibility in specification sheets.</p><figure data-bordeaux-image-check="" id="elk-739d8ade-b82a-11f1-8907-618cc1a71d6a"><div><p> <picture data-new-v2-image="true">
<source type="image/webp" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-1200-80.jpg.webp 1200w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-1024-80.jpg.webp 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-970-80.jpg.webp 970w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-650-80.jpg.webp 650w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-480-80.jpg.webp 480w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-320-80.jpg.webp 320w" sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 970px, calc(100vw - 40px)">
<img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K.jpg" alt="Surface CVP in front of a Surface logo" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-1200-80.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-1024-80.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-970-80.jpg 970w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-650-80.jpg 650w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-480-80.jpg 480w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K-320-80.jpg 320w" sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 970px, calc(100vw - 40px)" loading="lazy" data-new-v2-image="true" data-original-mos="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K.jpg" data-pin-media="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/g4vvzRaZZVpLb7BEwkfG8K.jpg">
</picture></p></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description"><span>Surface CVP Brett Ostrum announcing Surface PCs in 2024 at Copilot+ event. </span><span itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Microsoft)</span></figcaption></figure><p id="elk-739d8b60-b82a-11f1-926d-2fe55b432214">At this year's Snapdragon Summit, I had the chance to ask Surface CVP Brett Ostrum about the lack of Copilot+ PC branding on its <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/surface/microsoft-unveils-new-surface-pro-12-inch-and-surface-laptop-13-inch-with-snapdragon-x2-plus-brighter-screens-and-5g-options-heres-a-first-look" data-url="https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/surface/microsoft-unveils-new-surface-pro-12-inch-and-surface-laptop-13-inch-with-snapdragon-x2-plus-brighter-screens-and-5g-options-heres-a-first-look" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.windowscentral.com/hardware/surface/microsoft-unveils-new-surface-pro-12-inch-and-surface-laptop-13-inch-with-snapdragon-x2-plus-brighter-screens-and-5g-options-heres-a-first-look">recent Surface PCs</a>, and he confirmed that<em> "</em><em><strong>these are not called Copilot+ PCs.</strong></em><em> They do meet all the requirements of our previous bar for what Copilot+ devices are. We still lean into the narrative around AI on the edge and being able to have a hybrid solution out there."</em></p><p>I've also asked Qualcomm's SVP of compute, Kedar Kondap, about his thoughts on the usage of the Copilot+ PC branding. He told me that <em>"the purpose for Copilot+ PCs was to be able to deliver [NPU] experiences. So it was to define a certain category of devices with a certain bar and metric, like for example, a 45 TOPS NPU ... So from that perspective, it's more offering the same experiences, </em><em><strong>probably without just using [Copilot+ PC] terminology now.</strong></em><em>"</em></p><p>It's also worth mentioning that NVIDIA hasn't gone anywhere near the Copilot+ PC brand for its upcoming RTX Spark platform, even though all RTX Spark PCs meet the Copilot+ PC specification bar. I suspect that's a deliberate decision.</p><p>It seems pretty obvious that the Copilot+ PC brand hasn't resonated with the market, and OEMs and Microsoft itself are now quietly pulling back on that branding. The specification baseline for Copilot+ PC experiences still exists, it just no longer has a pretty marketing name tied to it.</p><p>The rough launch of Copilot+ PCs, paired with the fact that <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.windowscentral.com/artificial-intelligence/microsoft-copilot" data-auto-tag-linker="true" data-url="https://www.windowscentral.com/artificial-intelligence/microsoft-copilot" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-redirect="https://www.windowscentral.com/tag/microsoft-copilot" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.windowscentral.com/artificial-intelligence/microsoft-copilot">Microsoft Copilot</a> itself doesn't have a very strong reputation these days, likely contributed to the decision to slowly retire the Copilot+ PC branding. The brand never made sense to me anyway... these PCs have <em>nothing </em>to do with Copilot.</p><hr id="elk-99952964-b829-11f1-b304-f533926183fd"><a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/windowscentral/" id="elk-999529e6-b829-11f1-bc28-b3814ab122b0" data-url="https://www.reddit.com/r/windowscentral/" target="_blank" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="microsoft"><figure data-bordeaux-image-check=""><div><p> <picture data-new-v2-image="true">
